# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
debug
target

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb

# Stray rustc output when compiling from stdin (rustc --crate-type lib -)
librust_out.rlib

# Generated by cargo mutants
# Contains mutation testing data
**/mutants.out*/

# RustRover
#  JetBrains specific template is maintained in a separate JetBrains.gitignore that can
#  be found at https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Global/JetBrains.gitignore
#  and can be added to the global gitignore or merged into this file.  For a more nuclear
#  option (not recommended) you can uncomment the following to ignore the entire idea folder.
#.idea/
.DS_Store

# Secrets file (contains API keys)
secrets.json
secrets.enc

# Pre-built binaries for packaging (created during CI)
snap/local/
flatpak/bin/

# macOS app bundle (generated by build script)
*.app
*.dmg

# Generated test images (keep .zst compressed fixtures in repo)
tests/fixtures/*.img
outputs/
/target-cross
/buildroot/output
/buildroot/dl
/buildroot/ccache
/buildroot/overlay/usr/bin/rb-cli
/crusty-backup/dist
/crusty-backup/dl
/crusty-backup/net/dl
/crusty-backup/net/mtcp
/crusty-backup/net/watt32
# /crusty-backup/net/drivers/*.com are vendored now (Crynwr GPL set via
# net/fetch-drivers.sh) so the CI release media ships with packet drivers.

# Corpus-derived summaries (regenerated; keep only problematic_isos.json)
problematic_isos.tsv
problematic_isos.csv

# mrustc PowerPC build: vendored dep sources for the rb-cli-{vintage,ppc}
# engines, regenerated by `cargo vendor` (see scripts/build-ppc.sh /
# docs/build-ppc-mrustc.md).
rb-cli-*/vendor/
# Python bytecode from the scripts/ probe + build helpers
__pycache__/
# Packaged PowerPC bundle from `scripts/build-ppc.sh dist`
dist/
# local.toml carries this host's fixture root; local-devices.toml is the
# allowlist of physical devices the hardware tier is permitted to write to,
# and committing one would be actively dangerous on someone else's machine.
# Names real machines; hosts.toml.example is the committed template.
/regression-tests/data/hosts.toml
# Names real machines; hosts.local.example is the committed template.
/regression-tests/scripts/hosts.local
/regression-tests/local.toml
/regression-tests/local-devices.toml
# The NAS catalogue maps IDs to real corpus paths and stays on the NAS.
# fixture-map.repo.tsv is deliberately NOT ignored: it only references
# fixtures already committed under tests/ via a repo: prefix.
/regression-tests/fixture-map.tsv
/regression-tests/fixture-map.json
/regression-tests/fixtures/
/regression-tests/scratch/
/regression-tests/runs/
/regression-tests/scans/
# `produce` output. Binary, per-host, and regenerated on demand — the tree is
# synced between hosts by any means (NAS, rsync), never through git.
/regression-tests/artifacts/
# `verify` output. Per-verifier, regenerated on demand, same as artifacts/.
/regression-tests/verifications/
/regression-tests/runner/target/
regression-tests/**/*.local.toml

# data/regression.json is generated by `rb-regress export` but is deliberately
# COMMITTED: it is the normalised snapshot of the matrix, and having it in the
# tree means a coverage change shows up in a diff. Regenerate rather than edit.
